course: dictionary
lesson: 11523
title: Making bad things less bad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The verb alleviate means to make something bad less bad, or easier to bear. It is a formal word, so you will meet it more in serious writing, news and essays than in chatty talk.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «The doctor gave her tablets to alleviate the pain in her knee.» The pain is the bad thing, and the tablets make it less bad. So alleviate means to ease something that hurts or troubles you.
explain: You can alleviate many things, not only pain. «Food aid will alleviate hunger in the village.» «Games on the long journey alleviate boredom.» Each time, something unpleasant becomes easier to bear.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in a simpler word such as ease or lessen. If it still makes sense, you have understood alleviate well.
fact: Alleviate is a verb, so its forms are alleviate, alleviates, alleviated and alleviating.
fact: «Alleviate» means to make pain, a problem or suffering less bad or easier to bear.
fact: You can alleviate pain, poverty, hunger or boredom: «alleviate pain», «alleviate poverty», «alleviate boredom».
fact: Ease and lessen are simpler words that can often take the place of alleviate.
fact: Alleviate is formal, so you will often see it in health reports, news and essays.
exercise: «The cream will alleviate the itch on your arm.» What does alleviate mean? | To spread something far away | *To make something less bad | To hide something from view | To make something itch more
exercise: Ella says, «The medicine will alleviate my cough.» Which simple word is closest in meaning to alleviate? | *ease | enjoy | breathe | argue
exercise: «Food aid will alleviate hunger in the village.» What is being made less bad? | the village | the food | the aid workers | *hunger
test: «Clean water can alleviate suffering after a flood.» What does alleviate mean here? | To make the water dirty | To forget about the flood | *To make suffering less bad | To cause more suffering
test: Jayden says, «A writer uses alleviate because it is more formal than ease.» Is he right? | No, alleviate is a childish word | *Yes, alleviate is a more formal word | No, alleviate is only used about animals | No, alleviate means to make things worse
exam: «New laws aim to alleviate poverty in the city.» What is the aim of these laws? | To make poverty spread across the country | *To make poverty less bad | To count how many people are poor | To move poor families to the hills
